-- Replaces the live privacy policy text with the amended version (volunteer mobile numbers and T-shirt sizes, the public rota and points list).
-- Run once in the Supabase SQL editor. Any edits made in the site editor since are overwritten.
update public.site_settings set value = $privacy${"body_md": "## Who we are\n\nThe Yorkshire and North East Powerlifting Federation (YNEPF) runs powerlifting competitions and events in our region. We are responsible for the personal information described in this policy. In data protection terms, we are the \"controller\". The Federation's Chairperson looks after data protection.\n\nYou can contact us at info@ynepf.co.uk.\n\n## What information we collect\n\nMost of this comes from you, when you enter a competition or volunteer. We collect:\n\n- Your first and last name, and your date of birth.\n- Your age category, weight class and the region you compete in.\n- Your address.\n- Your sex, which we use only to enter you into the right competition category (men's or women's). This should usually match your sex at birth. If you are a transgender athlete, please contact us before you enter and we will talk it through. We may ask whether you hold a Therapeutic Use Exemption (TUE) for hormones you take, because that can affect which category you compete in.\n- Your email address and phone number, and other contact details. We use them to contact you about your entry and to fix any problems.\n- Your social media handles, and links to public profiles such as OpenPowerlifting or OpenIPF. If you give us an OpenIPF profile link, we read your public results from it, to check qualifying totals and to show your best total in the lifting order.\n- Payment information. Stripe takes payments for us. We don't store any bank or card details. Stripe shows us the last four digits of the card used.\n- Your competition results: your lifts, scores and placings.\n- For volunteers, your name and email address, an optional mobile number, your T-shirt size, which roles you can do, and which competitions you helped at. We also keep a record of the points you earn and spend.\n- Photos and video taken at our events and in livestreams.\n- If you join a competition's waiting list, your name and email address.\n- Messages you send us, and your email address if you join our mailing list.\n- Website information: we don't set any cookies of our own. Our website host and our payment provider (Stripe) may use cookies or collect their own information about visitors. That is covered by their own privacy policies, not this one.\n\nWe don't ask for health information.\n\n## Why we use your information\n\n- To run competitions, including entries, lifting orders, weigh-in, scoring and results. We need this to carry out your entry.\n- To contact you about your entry, and to chase or fix anything that's missing or wrong. This is in our legitimate interests, and yours.\n- To publish results and keep records of the sport. This is in our legitimate interests.\n- To thank volunteers and give them priority entry, discounts and other rewards for their points. This is in our legitimate interests.\n- To organise volunteers: the rota, a WhatsApp group for the competition if you give us your mobile number, and volunteer T-shirts. This is in our legitimate interests. We only use your mobile number if you choose to give it to us.\n- To livestream and photograph events, to sell photo packages, and to promote powerlifting. This is in our legitimate interests.\n- To send you emails about competitions you may want to enter. We do this with your consent, or where you have entered with us before. You can unsubscribe at any time.\n- To keep our financial records. This is a legal duty.\n- To keep events safe, including recording accidents. This is in our legitimate interests, and sometimes a legal duty.\n\n## Who can see it\n\nWe never sell your information. We don't share it with anyone else without your consent, except in the ways listed here or where the law says we must.\n\n- The YNEPF executive and committee. They can see the information you give us, including the more personal details.\n- Franchise hosts. A small number of people at the club or training facility running a competition can view the entrant details they need to coordinate entries and chase anyone who needs chasing. They don't hold their own copies, because the Federation keeps the information. Hosts must use these details only for that competition and keep them secure.\n- White Lights Media and the member who photographs for each division. They can see who has ordered and paid for a photo package. This is only so they can deliver it on time and keep things consistent. They must not share this information or use it for anything else.\n- Referees and officials working at the event, who need to see lifting orders.\n- Everyone, for volunteers: our public volunteer rota shows names and roles, and our points list shows names and points. We never publish volunteers' email addresses or mobile numbers.\n- British Powerlifting and the IPF, who receive results when we upload them to OpenIPF. We only upload results.\n- The services we use to run the website and our entries: Stripe (payments), Supabase (our secure database and sign-in), Resend (sending our emails), Vercel (hosting the website) and Google (our email accounts and shared documents). They handle information only on our behalf.\n- Anyone the law says we must tell, such as the police or a regulator.\n\nSome of these providers may handle information outside the UK. When they do, we rely on the safeguards that UK data protection law requires.\n\n## Results, photos and livestreams\n\nResults are public. We publish names, clubs, weight classes and lifts on our website and social media. We also upload results to OpenIPF, which is how British Powerlifting and the IPF receive them. OpenIPF is public. We don't publish your address, date of birth or contact details.\n\nWe photograph and film everyone at our events, and some events are livestreamed. There is no way to opt out, so by entering you agree to be photographed and filmed. When you enter, you also agree that we can use photos and video of you in our promotional material.\n\n## Under 18s\n\nFor lifters under 18, a parent or guardian enters them or gives permission. That permission covers photos, video and promotional material. We publish only their name, age category and lifts.\n\n## How long we keep it\n\n- Entry details, including your address and contact details: 24 months after the competition, then we delete them.\n- Volunteer details: 24 months.\n- Results: we keep these permanently as a record of the sport. They also stay on OpenIPF and OpenPowerlifting, which keep them indefinitely.\n- Payment and financial records: 6 years.\n- Accident records: 3 years, or longer for under 18s.\n- Mailing list details: until you unsubscribe.\n\n## Keeping it safe\n\nWe store your information in the secure online services listed above. Only people who need it can open it, and our editing tools need a sign-in. If you think your information has been shared when it shouldn't have been, tell us straight away at info@ynepf.co.uk.\n\n## Your rights\n\nYou can ask us to:\n\n- show you the information we hold about you\n- correct anything that is wrong\n- delete your information\n- limit or stop how we use it\n- give you a copy to move to another service, where this applies\n- stop using it if we rely on your consent, and you withdraw that consent\n\nEmail info@ynepf.co.uk to use any of these rights. We will reply within one month. Some rights have limits. For example, we may need to keep results as a record of the sport.\n\nIf you are unhappy with how we have handled your information, you can complain to the Information Commissioner's Office (ICO) at [ico.org.uk](https://ico.org.uk) or on 0303 123 1113. We would like the chance to put things right first.\n\n## Changes to this policy\n\nWe may update this policy. The latest version will be on our website, with the date at the top.\n", "updated": "2026-11-18"}$privacy$, updated_at = now() where key = 'page.privacy';
